According to a new report from Intel Market Research, the global Small Cylindrical Batteries for Electric Vehicles market was valued at USD 12,330 million in 2024 and is projected to reach USD 31,530 million by 2032, growing at a CAGR of 10.8% during the forecast period (2025–2032). This growth trajectory reflects the increasing adoption of electric mobility solutions worldwide and the critical role of cylindrical lithium-ion batteries in powering next-generation EVs.
What are Small Cylindrical Batteries?
Small Cylindrical Batteries are standardized lithium-ion cells with compact cylindrical form factors (typically ≤32mm diameter), engineered specifically for electric vehicles (EVs), hybrids (HEVs/PHEVs), and micromobility applications. These batteries strike an optimal balance between energy density, cost efficiency, and manufacturing scalability by leveraging mature production processes originally developed for consumer electronics. Their standardized design enables high-volume production while maintaining robust performance characteristics.
The technology has gained prominence through applications in leading EV models, particularly in Tesla's battery architecture, which utilizes thousands of 21700 cells per vehicle pack. This approach highlights how small cylindrical batteries deliver superior thermal management and safety features compared to other form factors.
